.TH SEMACQUIRE 2
.SH NAME
semacquire, semrelease \- user level semaphores
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "semacquire :: proc(sem: ^i64, block: bool) -> i64"
.B "semrelease :: proc(sem: ^i64, count: i64) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Semacquire
and
.I semrelease
schedule between processes sharing memory, arranged with
.B RFMEM
(see
.IR fork (2))
or
.IR segattach (2).
.PP
The semaphore's value is the eight-byte, eight-byte-aligned word at
.IR sem .
.I Semacquire
waits until that word is positive and then decrements it, answering 1.
If
.I block
is false and the word is not positive, it answers 0 instead of waiting.
It answers a negative
.B EINTR
if a note interrupted the wait, and a negative
.B EFAULT
if
.I sem
is not an address the caller may reach and write, or is not
eight-byte aligned.
.PP
.I Semrelease
adds
.I count
to the word and wakes every process waiting on it, answering zero.
.I Count
must be positive; unlike Plan 9's
.IR semrelease ,
the answer is not the semaphore's new value.
.PP
.I Semacquire
and
.I semrelease
are meant for coordinating processes, the way
.IR rendezvous (2)
is, and not threads within one process; a thread library built over
.B RFMEM
uses locks or channels of its own for that (see
.IR thread (2)).
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/user.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR rendezvous (2),
.IR segattach (2),
.IR thread (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers as described above; a negative answer is
.B \-errno ,
named by
.IR errstr (2).
